  "worked_solution": "The *universal quantification* of a predicate $P(x),$ defined over the universal set $U,$ transforms this predicate into a statement that we write as\n\n$$\n∀x\\in U,\\,P(x).\n$$\n\nThis statement is true if the predicate $P(x)$ is true on the entire universal set $U,$ i.e. if the truth set ${T}_{P}$ of the predicate coincides with the universal set $U:$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P}=U\n$$\n\nSince $\\frac{m}{2}$ is an integer if and only if $m$ is even, we have that the truth set of our predicate is ${T}_{P}={2k:k\\in Z}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I is false. The truth set is *not* empty.\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, ${T}_{P}={2k:k\\in Z}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${T}_{P}$ doesn't coincide with the universal set, the statement $∀m\\in Z,\\,P(m)$ is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
